---
import Landing from "../layouts/Landing.astro";
import EvidenceChart from "../components/EvidenceChart.astro";

const repo = "https://github.com/caioricciuti/ldf";

// `ldf-mcp convert` output for the JSON below, byte for byte. Token counts
// are `ldf-bench count` (o200k_base) on the LDF and on the JSON in compact
// form; the JSON is shown wrapped, one record per line.
const example = `@records id:s,customer.name:s,items:t,total:f,status:s
ORD-1,Val Thompson,2,715.94,shipped
ORD-2,Sol Ritchie,1,2757.03,cancelled
ORD-3,Kim Abara,2,53.78,shipped
@records.items id:s,sku:s,qty:i,price:f
ORD-1,E5,3,28.78
ORD-1,C3,4,157.4
ORD-2,A1,1,2757.03
ORD-3,B2,2,12.5
ORD-3,E5,1,28.78`;

const json = `[{"id":"ORD-1","customer":{"name":"Val Thompson"},
  "items":[{"sku":"E5","qty":3,"price":28.78},
           {"sku":"C3","qty":4,"price":157.4}],
  "total":715.94,"status":"shipped"},
 {"id":"ORD-2","customer":{"name":"Sol Ritchie"},
  "items":[{"sku":"A1","qty":1,"price":2757.03}],
  "total":2757.03,"status":"cancelled"},
 {"id":"ORD-3","customer":{"name":"Kim Abara"},
  "items":[{"sku":"B2","qty":2,"price":12.5},
           {"sku":"E5","qty":1,"price":28.78}],
  "total":53.78,"status":"shipped"}]`;

const install = `cargo install ldf-mcp --locked
ldf-mcp clickhouse --url http://127.0.0.1:8123 --user reader`;

// Copied from reports/2026-10-21-clickhouse (bench_mcp.py, ClickHouse 25.8,
// the medium fixtures): bytes of the tool result as JSON and as LDF, and
// o200k_base tokens of LDF as a share of JSON's.
const chRows = [
  ["SELECT * FROM users LIMIT 50", "50", "6 909", "3 134", "0.62"],
  ["SELECT * FROM orders LIMIT 100", "100", "29 465", "13 676", "0.56"],
  ["SELECT * FROM logs WHERE level IN (…) LIMIT 500", "500", "72 615", "34 687", "0.65"],
  ["SELECT * FROM wide LIMIT 1000", "1 000", "540 065", "274 626", "0.43"],
  ["SELECT * FROM events LIMIT 200", "200", "51 435", "39 107", "0.81"],
];

// The server commands as any MCP client takes them; each client has its own
// way to register a command.
const servers = `ldf-mcp clickhouse --url http://127.0.0.1:8123 --user reader
ldf-mcp files --root ./exports       # csv, tsv, json, jsonl, parquet`;

const proxy = `ldf-mcp proxy --model claude-opus-5-5 -- <your database MCP server> [its args]`;

const convert = `ldf-mcp convert export.parquet > export.ldf`;

const lib = `let text = ldf::encode(&records);   // &[ldf_data::Value]
let back = ldf::decode(&text)?;      // the same records`;

const report = `${repo}/tree/main/reports/2026-10-21-clickhouse`;
---

<Landing
  title="LDF: database results a language model reads for half the tokens"
  description="LDF puts query results in front of a language model as typed tables instead of JSON: lossless, about half the tokens, and given only to models measured to answer from it as well as from JSON."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container hero-grid">
      <div class="hero-copy" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">ldf-mcp 0.5 · spec v0.2</p>
        <h1 class="h1">Your database, read by a model, for half the tokens.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          When a model queries a database, the rows come back as JSON and every key is repeated on
          every row. LDF sends the same rows as typed tables: one header, one line per record, child
          tables for nested arrays. Nothing is lost. On held-out data Claude Opus 5.5 answers from LDF
          as well as from JSON, at 57 to 77 percent of JSON's input tokens, and a model without that
          kind of evidence keeps getting JSON.
        </p>
        <div class="sample install">
          <div class="sample-bar"><span>An MCP server for ClickHouse</span><span class="dim">one binary, read-only</span></div>
          <pre><code>{install}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="cta">
          <a class="btn btn-light" href="/docs/mcp/">Set it up <span class="arrow">→</span></a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={repo}>Source on GitHub</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-sample" data-reveal>
        <div class="sample">
          <div class="sample-bar"><span>orders.ldf</span><span class="dim">143 tokens</span></div>
          <pre><code>{example}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="sample faded">
          <div class="sample-bar"><span>the same data as JSON</span><span class="dim">169 tokens compact</span></div>
          <pre><code>{json}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p class="sample-note">Three records, so the headers weigh a lot. A hundred orders from ClickHouse come out at 0.56 of JSON's tokens, see below.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <ul class="container facts" data-reveal>
      <li><strong>Lossless.</strong> Byte-exact round trip on ten of twelve benchmark datasets; the two sparse ones come back with keys reordered, nothing lost.</li>
      <li><strong>Chosen per model.</strong> LDF goes only to a model measured reading it as well as JSON on that shape and size of data. Every other model keeps getting JSON.</li>
      <li><strong>Measured.</strong> Five models, held-out data, sign tests. The misses are in the reports, not hidden.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="demo">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">ClickHouse</p>
        <h2 class="h2">The queries an agent runs. <span class="soft">Same rows, fewer tokens.</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">
          <code>ldf-mcp clickhouse</code> against ClickHouse 25.8, each result taken once as LDF and once
          as JSON. No model in the loop: these are the bytes and tokens the model would be sent.
          Encoding adds under 2 ms per call up to 500 rows.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap" data-reveal>
        <table class="numbers">
          <thead><tr><th>query</th><th>rows</th><th>JSON bytes</th><th>LDF bytes</th><th>tokens, LDF / JSON</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {chRows.map(([q, rows, jb, lb, ratio]) => (
              <tr><td><code>{q}</code></td><td>{rows}</td><td>{jb}</td><td>{lb}</td><td class="strong">{ratio}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="note" data-reveal>o200k_base tokens. The orders come back with a nested customer and an <code>items</code> array; LDF writes them as a parent table and a child table, where TOON falls back to a list larger than JSON (1.07). <a href={report}>Report and reproduction</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="fit">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Where it fits</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Record sets, not everything.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="fit" data-reveal>
        <div class="fit-col">
          <h3>Helps</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Query results from databases and warehouses: the same columns on every row is exactly what JSON pays for over and over.</li>
            <li>API responses that are lists of records.</li>
            <li>Data files handed to a model: CSV, JSONL, Parquet exports.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="fit-col dim-col">
          <h3>Does not</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Coding agents. Their tool output is mostly source code, diffs and logs; in our own coding sessions record sets were a small share of it.</li>
            <li>Small results. Under a kilobyte the format note costs more than it saves, so they pass through as JSON.</li>
            <li>Prose, single objects, error messages. Nothing to tabulate.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="numbers">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Evidence</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Same questions, JSON against LDF.</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          Every model and dataset shape gets its own verdict: at least 50 graded questions, no more than
          3 points below JSON, at least 5 percent fewer tokens. Evidence also has a size band: an input
          is judged by the evaluation at the smallest measured size at or above its row count, so a
          result that passed at 200 records and failed at 1 000 goes out as LDF up to 200 rows and as
          JSON above.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="chart-wrap" data-reveal>
        <EvidenceChart dark />
      </div>
      <div class="stats" data-reveal>
        <div class="stat"><span class="stat-n">294 / 297</span><span class="stat-l">Claude Opus 5.5 on held-out data, LDF and JSON alike, with LDF at 57 to 77 percent of JSON's input tokens. <a href="/docs/results/stage-c/">Stage C</a></span></div>
        <div class="stat"><span class="stat-n">53 / 53</span><span class="stat-l">GPT-6.1 Sol on LDF at 50 records, zero-shot: the prompt names the format and shows the data, nothing more.</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><span class="stat-n">0.36 to 0.80</span><span class="stat-l">LDF's o200k tokens as a share of compact JSON on the first 1 000 records of six benchmark datasets. CSV ties it on flat data and cannot hold the rest. <a href="/docs/results/baseline/">Baseline</a></span></div>
      </div>
      <p class="note" data-reveal>Below the frontier tier no format is reliably better than another, and the reports say so. Read the <a href="/docs/methodology/">methodology</a> before quoting any number.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="use">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Use it</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Put it between the data and the model.</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          LDF is plain text in a prompt: no SDK, no model support needed. The tools and scripts on the
          other side keep seeing JSON; only the model's copy changes, and only when it comes out shorter.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="agent-samples" data-reveal>
        <div class="sample">
          <div class="sample-bar"><span>MCP servers</span><span class="dim">ClickHouse, or a directory of data files</span></div>
          <pre><code>{servers}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="sample">
          <div class="sample-bar"><span>In front of any database MCP server</span><span class="dim">stdio proxy</span></div>
          <pre><code>{proxy}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="sample">
          <div class="sample-bar"><span>Any pipeline or model API</span><span class="dim">json, jsonl, csv, tsv, parquet in</span></div>
          <pre><code>{convert}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="sample">
          <div class="sample-bar"><span>Rust</span><span class="dim">the ldf crate</span></div>
          <pre><code>{lib}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="mcp-points" data-reveal>
        <div><strong>Per model.</strong> Give it the model id and it checks the measured evidence. No evidence, no conversion.</div>
        <div><strong>Primer once.</strong> The model gets a short description of the format the first time, then the data.</div>
        <div><strong>Only what helps.</strong> Small results, errors, partial output and anything not a complete dataset pass through untouched.</div>
        <div><strong>Shell output too.</strong> An optional Claude Code hook converts JSON printed by shell commands. <a href="/docs/agents/">Agents guide</a></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="format">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">The format</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Eight rules. <span class="soft">That is the whole spec.</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">A document is a sequence of tables. Each table is a header line and rows. Everything else follows from the data.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rules" data-reveal>
        <div class="rule"><code>@records a:i,b:s</code><p>A header names the table and types each column: <code>i</code> integer, <code>f</code> float, <code>s</code> string, <code>b</code> boolean, <code>j</code> any JSON, <code>t</code> child table. <code>?</code> marks a column that may be null or absent.</p></div>
        <div class="rule"><code>1,hello</code><p>A row is one record, cells separated by commas. Strings are bare unless they need quoting, then they are JSON strings.</p></div>
        <div class="rule"><code>,</code> and <code>null</code><p>An empty cell means the key is absent. <code>null</code> means it is present and null. JSON's distinction survives.</p></div>
        <div class="rule"><code>customer.name:s</code><p>Objects with a stable shape flatten into dotted columns. Irregular ones stay as a JSON cell.</p></div>
        <div class="rule"><code>@records.items</code><p>Arrays of objects become a child table joined on the parent's first column. The parent keeps a count.</p></div>
        <div class="rule"><code>items:[sku,qty]</code><p>New in v0.2, opt-in: an array of flat objects with one key order stays in its row as lists of values, keys once in the header. 17 percent fewer tokens on nested orders.</p></div>
        <div class="rule"><code>~:j?</code><p>Keys present in fewer than half the rows travel in a small per-row object instead of a mostly empty column. Measured to matter for smaller models.</p></div>
        <div class="rule"><code>1.0</code> stays <code>1.0</code><p>Numbers keep their kind and shortest round-trip text. Integers above 2^53 do not get mangled.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="cta" data-reveal>
        <a class="btn btn-light" href="/docs/format/">The format in five minutes <span class="arrow">→</span></a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/docs/spec/">Specification</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="status">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Status</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Spec v0.2, ldf-mcp 0.5. <span class="soft">Evidence attached.</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">
          One Rust workspace, published on crates.io: the codec, the per-model selector,
          <code>ldf-mcp</code>, ten baseline formats implemented faithfully (TOON passes its official
          conformance suite), and the benchmark and evaluation harnesses. Every dependency pinned.
          ClickHouse and data files are supported directly; other databases through the proxy.
        </p>
        <p class="consult">
          Putting a model on top of your own database? Caio Ricciuti, who builds LDF, helps teams wire
          it up and measure it on their data: <a href="mailto:me@caioricciuti.com">me@caioricciuti.com</a>.
        </p>
        <div class="cta">
          <a class="btn btn-light" href="/docs/">Read the docs <span class="arrow">→</span></a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/docs/roadmap/">What comes next</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Landing>

<style>
  .hero {
